<div class="container"><div class="lesson-body">
<h2>p-adic Analysis — p-adic Numbers, Analytic Functions & the Fourier Transform</h2>
<p>Deep dive into p-adic Analysis — the field ℚ_p of p-adic numbers (completion of ℚ under the p-adic absolute value), p-adic integers ℤ_p, Hensel's lemma (lifting roots), p-adic analytic functions (power series converging on ℤ_p), the Mahler basis and the Amice transform, the p-adic Fourier transform (the Stone-von Neumann theorem in the p-adic setting), the p-adic gamma function (Morita), p-adic L-functions (Kubota-Leopoldt, Iwasawa theory), the Banach space structure of C(ℤ_p, ℚ_p), and applications to Iwasawa theory and p-adic Hodge theory.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"p-adic numbers are the numbers that live in the completions of ℚ other than ℝ."</div><div class="attribution">— Kurt Hensel</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 p-adic Numbers and the p-adic Absolute Value</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Foundations</div><div class="story-title">p-adic Valuations, Ultrametrics, and Hensel's Lemma</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>p-adic valuation</strong> v_p: ℚ → ℤ ∪ {∞} is defined by v_p(p^k·a/b) = k where p ∤ a, b. The <strong>p-adic absolute value</strong> |x|_p = p^{-v_p(x)} satisfies the ultrametric triangle inequality: |x+y|_p ≤ max(|x|_p, |y|_p). The field <strong>ℚ_p</strong> is the completion of ℚ under |·|_p. The ring of <strong>p-adic integers</strong> ℤ_p = {x ∈ ℚ_p : |x|_p ≤ 1} = lim← ℤ/pⁿℤ — an inverse limit of finite rings. Every x ∈ ℚ_p has a unique <strong>p-adic expansion</strong> x = Σ_{k=n}^∞ a_k p^k with a_k ∈ {0, …, p−1}. <strong>Hensel's lemma</strong>: if f ∈ ℤ_p[x] and f(a) ≡ 0 mod p, f'(a) not ≡ 0 mod p, then there exists a unique b ∈ ℤ_p with f(b) = 0 and b ≡ a mod p — the key tool for lifting roots. The field <strong>ℂ_p</strong> = completion of the algebraic closure of ℚ_p is both algebraically closed and complete.</div><div class="story-moral">The p-adic numbers form a complete ultrametric field offering a fundamentally different notion of distance, where two numbers are close if their difference is divisible by a high power of p.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 p-adic Analytic Functions</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Analysis</div><div class="story-title">Power Series on ℤ_p, the Mahler Basis, and the Amice Transform</div><div class="story-text">A function f: ℤ_p → ℚ_p is <strong>analytic</strong> if it has a power series expansion f(x) = Σ a_n x^n converging everywhere on ℤ_p (i.e., |a_n|_p → 0). The <strong>Mahler basis</strong>: every continuous function f: ℤ_p → ℚ_p has a unique representation f(x) = Σ a_n (x choose n) with |a_n|_p → 0, where (x choose n) = x(x−1)…(x−n+1)/n!. The Mahler coefficients are given by a_n = (Δⁿ f)(0) — the nth finite difference at 0. The <strong>Amice transform</strong> provides a correspondence between locally analytic functions and measures on ℤ_p via the Fourier transform on ℤ_p. The <strong>p-adic Fourier transform</strong>: for a character χ: ℤ_p → ℂ_p^×, the Fourier transform of a measure μ is ̂μ(χ) = ∫ χ(x) dμ(x). This parallels classical Fourier analysis but with the p-adic topology, leading to the p-adic Stone-von Neumann theorem and representations of the p-adic Heisenberg group.</div><div class="story-moral">p-adic analysis replaces the real topology with an ultrametric one; Mahler's basis using binomial coefficient polynomials is the p-adic analogue of the Taylor series.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 p-adic Gamma and Zeta Functions</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Special Functions</div><div class="story-title">Morita's p-adic Gamma, Kubota-Leopoldt L-functions, and Iwasawa Theory</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Morita p-adic gamma function</strong> Γ_p: ℤ_p → ℤ_p is defined by Γ_p(n) = (−1)ⁿ ∏_{k=1, p∤k}^{n−1} k for n ∈ ℕ, extended continuously to ℤ_p. It satisfies Γ_p(x+1) = h_p(x)·Γ_p(x) where h_p(x) = −x if x ∈ ℤ_p^× and h_p(x) = −1 if x ∈ pℤ_p. The <strong>p-adic L-functions</strong>: Kubota-Leopoldt L_p(s, χ) for Dirichlet characters χ interpolate the values L(1−n, χω⁻ⁿ) for n ≥ 1, where ω is the Teichmüller character — these are p-adic meromorphic functions on the p-adic unit disk. The <strong>Iwasawa main conjecture</strong> (proved by Mazur-Wiles for ℚ, Wiles for totally real fields) relates p-adic L-functions to the characteristic ideals of class groups in cyclotomic ℤ_p-extensions: it asserts an equality of ideals in the Iwasawa algebra Λ = ℤ_p[[T]]. This is the central pillar of <strong>Iwasawa theory</strong>, connecting p-adic zeta values to arithmetic invariants of number fields.</div><div class="story-moral">p-adic L-functions interpolate classical L-values at negative integers and encode deep arithmetic information about class groups via Iwasawa theory.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 p-adic Hodge Theory</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Hodge Theory</div><div class="story-title">Fontaine's Period Rings, Crystalline Representations, and Perfectoid Spaces</div><div class="story-text"><strong>p-adic Hodge theory</strong> compares p-adic étale cohomology with algebraic de Rham cohomology for varieties over p-adic fields. <strong>Fontaine's period rings</strong> — B_{HT}, B_{st}, B_{dR}, B_{cris} — are topological ℚ_p-algebras equipped with Galois actions and filtrations. A p-adic representation V of Gal(̄ℚ_p/ℚ_p) is <strong>de Rham</strong> if dim_{ℚ_p} (V ⊗_{ℚ_p} B_{dR})^{Gal} = dim_{ℚ_p} V; it is <strong>crystalline</strong> if it comes from étale cohomology of a variety with good reduction (equivalently, V is de Rham and the associated filtered φ-module is admissible); and <strong>semistable</strong> if from a variety with semistable reduction. The <strong>Fargues-Fontaine curve</strong> is a complete algebraic curve whose closed points correspond to equivalence classes of untilts of perfectoid fields — the curve whose geometry controls p-adic representations via the <strong>Kisin-Ren theory</strong> of étale φ-modules. <strong>Scholze's perfectoid spaces</strong> and the <strong>tilting correspondence</strong>: for a perfectoid field K, its tilt K^♭ is a perfectoid field of characteristic p establishing an equivalence of étale sites. Applications include the <strong>local Langlands correspondence</strong> for GL_n via <strong>Fargues-Scholze geometric Langlands</strong> on the Fargues-Fontaine curve and advances in the p-adic Langlands program.</div><div class="story-moral">p-adic Hodge theory bridges geometry and arithmetic through period rings, revealing that the p-adic cohomology of varieties is controlled by linear-algebraic data.</div></div>
